# test/bundle-current.test.cjs guards the two committed bundles with a
# byte-exact comparison:
#
#   assert.strictEqual(fs.readFileSync(OUT, 'utf8'), build())
#
# `build()` concatenates the source files as they sit on disk, so that
# comparison only holds when the bundle and its sources have the SAME line
# endings. On Windows, core.autocrlf=true checks everything out as CRLF, which
# happens to be self-consistent - but it makes the suite's result depend on a
# per-developer git setting rather than on the repository, and it breaks the
# moment one side is pinned and the other is not.
#
# Pinning every JS file to LF removes that dependency: the working copy then
# matches the blobs git already stores (git normalises to LF internally), so
# `build()` reads LF, writes LF, and the checked-out bundle is LF too, on every
# platform and whatever autocrlf says.
#
# This produces no commit diff. The stored blobs are already LF; the only thing
# that changes is what lands in the working copy on checkout.
*.js       text eol=lf
*.cjs      text eol=lf
*.json     text eol=lf
